    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56da",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "56DA Minister may recognise external dispute resolution schemes",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-56da"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.109",
      "values:originalText": "Recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(1) The Minister may, by notifiable instrument, recognise an external\ndispute resolution scheme for the resolution of disputes:\n\n(a) relating to the operation of the consumer data rules, or this\nPart, in relation to one or more designated sectors or types of\nCDR actions; and\n\n(b) involving one or more of the following:\n\n(i) CDR participants for CDR data;\n\n(ii) CDR consumers for CDR data;\n\n(iii) designated gateways for CDR data;\n\n(iiia) CDR action participants;\n\n(iiib) CDR consumers for CDR actions;\n\n(iv) other persons relating to any of those designated sectors\nor types of CDR actions.\nNote 1:\nThe consumer data rules may require internal dispute resolution\nschemes, see paragraph 56BJ(g).\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) The Minister may, in the instrument under subsection (1):\n\n(a) specify a period for which the recognition of the external\ndispute resolution scheme is in force; and\n\n(b) make the recognition of the external dispute resolution\nscheme subject to specified conditions, including conditions\nrelating to the conduct of an independent review of the\noperation of the scheme.\nBefore recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(3) Before recognising an external dispute resolution scheme under\nsubsection (1), the Minister must consider:\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.",
      "skos:definition": "Recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(1) The Minister may, by notifiable instrument, recognise an external\ndispute resolution scheme for the resolution of disputes:\n\n(a) relating to the operation of the consumer data rules, or this\nPart, in relation to one or more designated sectors or types of\nCDR actions; and\n\n(b) involving one or more of the following:\n\n(i) CDR participants for CDR data;\n\n(ii) CDR consumers for CDR data;\n\n(iii) designated gateways for CDR data;\n\n(iiia) CDR action participants;\n\n(iiib) CDR consumers for CDR actions;\n\n(iv) other persons relating to any of those designated sectors\nor types of CDR actions.\nNote 1:\nThe consumer data rules may require internal dispute resolution\nschemes, see paragraph 56BJ(g).\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) The Minister may, in the instrument under subsection (1):\n\n(a) specify a period for which the recognition of the external\ndispute resolution scheme is in force; and\n\n(b) make the recognition of the external dispute resolution\nscheme subject to specified conditions, including conditions\nrelating to the conduct of an independent review of the\noperation of the scheme.\nBefore recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(3) Before recognising an external dispute resolution scheme under\nsubsection (1), the Minister must consider:\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56da-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56da-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56da-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "9b70d39b25d09c575b0752f82f3ca483de42cb65cad9365dec64779c2c3a1dcb"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-56da",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "56DA Minister may recognise external dispute resolution schemes",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 109,
      "values:originalText": "Recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(1) The Minister may, by notifiable instrument, recognise an external\ndispute resolution scheme for the resolution of disputes:\n\n(a) relating to the operation of the consumer data rules, or this\nPart, in relation to one or more designated sectors or types of\nCDR actions; and\n\n(b) involving one or more of the following:\n\n(i) CDR participants for CDR data;\n\n(ii) CDR consumers for CDR data;\n\n(iii) designated gateways for CDR data;\n\n(iiia) CDR action participants;\n\n(iiib) CDR consumers for CDR actions;\n\n(iv) other persons relating to any of those designated sectors\nor types of CDR actions.\nNote 1:\nThe consumer data rules may require internal dispute resolution\nschemes, see paragraph 56BJ(g).\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) The Minister may, in the instrument under subsection (1):\n\n(a) specify a period for which the recognition of the external\ndispute resolution scheme is in force; and\n\n(b) make the recognition of the external dispute resolution\nscheme subject to specified conditions, including conditions\nrelating to the conduct of an independent review of the\noperation of the scheme.\nBefore recognising an external dispute resolution scheme\n\n(3) Before recognising an external dispute resolution scheme under\nsubsection (1), the Minister must consider:\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc."
    },
