    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56gc",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "56GC Complying with CDR requirements etc.: protection from",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-56gc"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.160",
      "values:originalText": "liability\n\n(1) If a person (the CDR entity), acting as described in an item of the\nfollowing table, does something mentioned in that item:\n\n(a) in good faith; and\n\n(b) in compliance with the CDR provisions; and\n\n(c) in compliance with each law (if any) of the Commonwealth,\nof a State or of a Territory prescribed by the regulations;\nthe CDR entity is not liable to an action or other proceeding,\nwhether civil or criminal, for or in relation to the thing in that item.\n\nDoing a CDR thing in good faith in compliance with the CDR provisions etc.\nItem\nWhen acting:\nthe things are:\n(a) as a data holder of CDR data; or\n(b) as an accredited data recipient of\nCDR data; or\n(c) as a designated gateway for CDR\ndata\n(a) providing the data to another\nperson; or\n(b) otherwise allowing another\nperson access to the data.\nas an accredited action initiator for a\ntype of CDR action\ngiving an instruction that is a valid\ninstruction for the performance of a\nCDR action of that type.\nas an action service provider for a\ntype of CDR action\nprocessing an instruction that is a\nvalid instruction for the performance\nof a CDR action of that type.\nNote 1:\nItem 3 of the table is focussing on the instruction layer not the action\nlayer, and so does not cover performance of the CDR action.\nNote 2:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this\nsubsection for a criminal action or criminal proceeding (see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nConsumer data right  Part IVD\nOther matters  Division 7\n\nSection 56GD\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) A person who wishes to rely on subsection (1) in relation to a civil\naction or civil proceeding bears an evidential burden in relation to\nthat matter.\n\n(3) In this section:\nevidential burden, in relation to a matter, means the burden of\nadducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable\npossibility that the matter exists or does not exist.",
      "skos:definition": "liability\n\n(1) If a person (the CDR entity), acting as described in an item of the\nfollowing table, does something mentioned in that item:\n\n(a) in good faith; and\n\n(b) in compliance with the CDR provisions; and\n\n(c) in compliance with each law (if any) of the Commonwealth,\nof a State or of a Territory prescribed by the regulations;\nthe CDR entity is not liable to an action or other proceeding,\nwhether civil or criminal, for or in relation to the thing in that item.\n\nDoing a CDR thing in good faith in compliance with the CDR provisions etc.\nItem\nWhen acting:\nthe things are:\n(a) as a data holder of CDR data; or\n(b) as an accredited data recipient of\nCDR data; or\n(c) as a designated gateway for CDR\ndata\n(a) providing the data to another\nperson; or\n(b) otherwise allowing another\nperson access to the data.\nas an accredited action initiator for a\ntype of CDR action\ngiving an instruction that is a valid\ninstruction for the performance of a\nCDR action of that type.\nas an action service provider for a\ntype of CDR action\nprocessing an instruction that is a\nvalid instruction for the performance\nof a CDR action of that type.\nNote 1:\nItem 3 of the table is focussing on the instruction layer not the action\nlayer, and so does not cover performance of the CDR action.\nNote 2:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this\nsubsection for a criminal action or criminal proceeding (see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nConsumer data right  Part IVD\nOther matters  Division 7\n\nSection 56GD\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) A person who wishes to rely on subsection (1) in relation to a civil\naction or civil proceeding bears an evidential burden in relation to\nthat matter.\n\n(3) In this section:\nevidential burden, in relation to a matter, means the burden of\nadducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable\npossibility that the matt",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56gc-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56gc-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-56gc-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "c5fd94b8c335618802e86694c5bfc293a5bc68c6200fe46c550cf2b24ac595f3"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-56gc",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "56GC Complying with CDR requirements etc.: protection from",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 160,
      "values:originalText": "liability\n\n(1) If a person (the CDR entity), acting as described in an item of the\nfollowing table, does something mentioned in that item:\n\n(a) in good faith; and\n\n(b) in compliance with the CDR provisions; and\n\n(c) in compliance with each law (if any) of the Commonwealth,\nof a State or of a Territory prescribed by the regulations;\nthe CDR entity is not liable to an action or other proceeding,\nwhether civil or criminal, for or in relation to the thing in that item.\n\nDoing a CDR thing in good faith in compliance with the CDR provisions etc.\nItem\nWhen acting:\nthe things are:\n(a) as a data holder of CDR data; or\n(b) as an accredited data recipient of\nCDR data; or\n(c) as a designated gateway for CDR\ndata\n(a) providing the data to another\nperson; or\n(b) otherwise allowing another\nperson access to the data.\nas an accredited action initiator for a\ntype of CDR action\ngiving an instruction that is a valid\ninstruction for the performance of a\nCDR action of that type.\nas an action service provider for a\ntype of CDR action\nprocessing an instruction that is a\nvalid instruction for the performance\nof a CDR action of that type.\nNote 1:\nItem 3 of the table is focussing on the instruction layer not the action\nlayer, and so does not cover performance of the CDR action.\nNote 2:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in this\nsubsection for a criminal action or criminal proceeding (see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nConsumer data right  Part IVD\nOther matters  Division 7\n\nSection 56GD\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(2) A person who wishes to rely on subsection (1) in relation to a civil\naction or civil proceeding bears an evidential burden in relation to\nthat matter.\n\n(3) In this section:\nevidential burden, in relation to a matter, means the burden of\nadducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable\npossibility that the matter exists or does not exist."
    },
