    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58BU Relationship with other duties and obligations",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58bu"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.218",
      "values:originalText": "A duty of confidence owed under an agreement or arrangement is\nof no effect to the extent that it is contrary to section 58BR or\n58BS.\nNote:\nEach of sections 58BR and 58BS is also a requirement by law to\ndisclose the information contained in the report referred to in that\nsection. So, complying with that section can be a defence to a secrecy\nprovision such as section 276 of the Telecommunications Act 1997\n(see paragraph 280(1)(b) of that Act).",
      "skos:definition": "A duty of confidence owed under an agreement or arrangement is\nof no effect to the extent that it is contrary to section 58BR or\n58BS.\nNote:\nEach of sections 58BR and 58BS is also a requirement by law to\ndisclose the information contained in the report referred to in that\nsection. So, complying with that section can be a defence to a secrecy\nprovision such as section 276 of the Telecommunications Act 1997\n(see paragraph 280(1)(b) of that Act).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1dffeb7292ec018e29d88e2d821fe560f0d83bc54ea51fb7794538ad90e4b331",
      "skos:note": "Duty of confidence is overridden by conflicting obligations."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58bu",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58BU Relationship with other duties and obligations",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 218,
      "values:originalText": "A duty of confidence owed under an agreement or arrangement is\nof no effect to the extent that it is contrary to section 58BR or\n58BS.\nNote:\nEach of sections 58BR and 58BS is also a requirement by law to\ndisclose the information contained in the report referred to in that\nsection. So, complying with that section can be a defence to a secrecy\nprovision such as section 276 of the Telecommunications Act 1997\n(see paragraph 280(1)(b) of that Act)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bu-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "overriddenBy",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Obligation override",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "Duty of confidence",
      "cml:conclusionText": "Conflicting obligation takes precedence"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bv",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58BV SPF general regulator may share information relating to",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58bv"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.218",
      "values:originalText": "scamming actions with relevant entities\n\n(1) The SPF general regulator may disclose information relating to\neither of the following actions (a scamming action):\n\n(a) a scam (as defined in section 58AG);\n\n(b) a scam (within the ordinary meaning of that expression);\nto an entity mentioned in subsection (2).\nNote 1:\nThis includes disclosing SPF personal information, but such\ninformation may first need to be de-identified (see subsection (4)).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nOverarching principles of the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 2\n\nSection 58BV\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nNote 2:\nThe SPF general regulator can also disclose the information to an SPF\nsector regulator (see section 58EG).\n\n(2) The entities are as follows:\n\n(a) a regulated entity;\n\n(b) a Commonwealth agency or authority involved in developing\nGovernment policy relating to this Part;\n\n(c) a law enforcement agency of the Commonwealth, or of a\nState or Territory;\n\n(d) an agency of a foreign country, or of part of a foreign\ncountry, that:\n\n(i) is a law enforcement agency; or\n\n(ii) is a regulatory agency responsible for scam prevention;\n\nif subsection (3) applies to a disclosure of information to the\nagency.\n\n(3) This subsection applies to a disclosure of information to a foreign\nagency if the SPF general regulator is satisfied that:\n\n(a) the agency has given an undertaking for the following:\n\n(i) controlling the storage and handling of the information;\n\n(ii) controlling the use that will be made of the information;\n\n(iii) ensuring that the information will be used only for the\npurpose for which it is disclosed to the agency; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate, in all the circumstances, to disclose the\ninformation to the agency.\n\n(4) SPF personal information may be disclosed under subsection (1).\nHowever, for a disclosure to an entity mentioned in\nparagraph (2)(b) such information must be de-identified unless the\nSPF general regulator reasonably believes that doing so would not\nachieve the object of this Part.\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.",
      "skos:definition": "scamming actions with relevant entities\n\n(1) The SPF general regulator may disclose information relating to\neither of the following actions (a scamming action):\n\n(a) a scam (as defined in section 58AG);\n\n(b) a scam (within the ordinary meaning of that expression);\nto an entity mentioned in subsection (2).\nNote 1:\nThis includes disclosing SPF personal information, but such\ninformation may first need to be de-identified (see subsection (4)).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nOverarching principles of the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 2\n\nSection 58BV\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nNote 2:\nThe SPF general regulator can also disclose the information to an SPF\nsector regulator (see section 58EG).\n\n(2) The entities are as follows:\n\n(a) a regulated entity;\n\n(b) a Commonwealth agency or authority involved in developing\nGovernment policy relating to this Part;\n\n(c) a law enforcement agency of the Commonwealth, or of a\nState or Territory;\n\n(d) an agency of a foreign country, or of part of a foreign\ncountry, that:\n\n(i) is a law enforcement agency; or\n\n(ii) is a regulatory agency responsible for scam prevention;\n\nif subsection (3) applies to a disclosure of information to the\nagency.\n\n(3) This subsection applies to a disclosure of information to a foreign\nagency if the SPF general regulator is satisfied that:\n\n(a) the agency has given an undertaking for the following:\n\n(i) controlling the storage and handling of the information;\n\n(ii) controlling the use that will be made of the information;\n\n(iii) ensuring that the information will be used only for the\npurpose for which it is disclosed to the agency; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate, in all the circumstances, to disclose the\ninformation to the agency.\n\n(4) SPF personal information may be disclosed under subsection (1).\nHowever, for a disclosure to an entity mentioned in\nparagraph (2)(b) such information must ",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bv-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bv-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bv-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58bv-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "210de191b58f276f6dfa68fec7904c1f6c4d77d849ccf41009604e3a1f8a5054"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58bv",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58BV SPF general regulator may share information relating to",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 218,
      "values:originalText": "scamming actions with relevant entities\n\n(1) The SPF general regulator may disclose information relating to\neither of the following actions (a scamming action):\n\n(a) a scam (as defined in section 58AG);\n\n(b) a scam (within the ordinary meaning of that expression);\nto an entity mentioned in subsection (2).\nNote 1:\nThis includes disclosing SPF personal information, but such\ninformation may first need to be de-identified (see subsection (4)).\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nOverarching principles of the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 2\n\nSection 58BV\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nNote 2:\nThe SPF general regulator can also disclose the information to an SPF\nsector regulator (see section 58EG).\n\n(2) The entities are as follows:\n\n(a) a regulated entity;\n\n(b) a Commonwealth agency or authority involved in developing\nGovernment policy relating to this Part;\n\n(c) a law enforcement agency of the Commonwealth, or of a\nState or Territory;\n\n(d) an agency of a foreign country, or of part of a foreign\ncountry, that:\n\n(i) is a law enforcement agency; or\n\n(ii) is a regulatory agency responsible for scam prevention;\n\nif subsection (3) applies to a disclosure of information to the\nagency.\n\n(3) This subsection applies to a disclosure of information to a foreign\nagency if the SPF general regulator is satisfied that:\n\n(a) the agency has given an undertaking for the following:\n\n(i) controlling the storage and handling of the information;\n\n(ii) controlling the use that will be made of the information;\n\n(iii) ensuring that the information will be used only for the\npurpose for which it is disclosed to the agency; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate, in all the circumstances, to disclose the\ninformation to the agency.\n\n(4) SPF personal information may be disclosed under subsection (1).\nHowever, for a disclosure to an entity mentioned in\nparagraph (2)(b) such information must be de-identified unless the\nSPF general regulator reasonably believes that doing so would not\nachieve the object of this Part.\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc."
    },
    {
