    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58da-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Permit",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Authorise external dispute resolution schemes for consumer complaints about scams.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58DB Minister may authorise external dispute resolution schemes",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58db"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.234",
      "values:originalText": "for a regulated sector\n\n(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, authorise an external\ndispute resolution scheme (an SPF EDR scheme) for the purposes\nof this Part and one or more regulated sectors if:\n\n(a) the scheme is already authorised under a Commonwealth law\nfor another purpose; or\n\n(b) the Minister is satisfied that the requirements prescribed by\nthe SPF rules for the purposes of subsection 58DC(1) are met\nfor the scheme.\nNote 1:\nFor paragraph (a), the Minister could, for example, authorise the\nAFCA scheme (within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001) to\napply for the purposes of this Part and a regulated sector. If that\nhappens, ASIC’s functions and powers relating to the AFCA scheme\n(for example, under section 1052A of that Act) will also apply for the\npurposes of this Part and the regulated sector.\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) Before authorising a scheme, the Minister must consider:\n\n(a) the accessibility of the scheme; and\n\n(b) the independence of the scheme; and\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nExternal dispute resolution for the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 4\n\nSection 58DC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(c) the fairness of the scheme; and\n\n(d) the accountability of the scheme; and\n\n(e) the efficiency of the scheme; and\n\n(f) the effectiveness of the scheme; and\n\n(g) any other matters the Minister considers relevant.\nA failure to comply with this subsection does not invalidate an\ninstrument made under subsection (1) authorising the scheme.\n\n(3) An instrument made under subsection (1) may make the\nauthorisation of the scheme subject to specified conditions.\n\n(4) An instrument made under subsection (1) authorising a scheme for\nwhich paragraph (1)(b) applies must set out the scheme.\n\n(5) More than one scheme may be authorised under subsection (1).",
      "skos:definition": "for a regulated sector\n\n(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, authorise an external\ndispute resolution scheme (an SPF EDR scheme) for the purposes\nof this Part and one or more regulated sectors if:\n\n(a) the scheme is already authorised under a Commonwealth law\nfor another purpose; or\n\n(b) the Minister is satisfied that the requirements prescribed by\nthe SPF rules for the purposes of subsection 58DC(1) are met\nfor the scheme.\nNote 1:\nFor paragraph (a), the Minister could, for example, authorise the\nAFCA scheme (within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001) to\napply for the purposes of this Part and a regulated sector. If that\nhappens, ASIC’s functions and powers relating to the AFCA scheme\n(for example, under section 1052A of that Act) will also apply for the\npurposes of this Part and the regulated sector.\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) Before authorising a scheme, the Minister must consider:\n\n(a) the accessibility of the scheme; and\n\n(b) the independence of the scheme; and\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nExternal dispute resolution for the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 4\n\nSection 58DC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(c) the fairness of the scheme; and\n\n(d) the accountability of the scheme; and\n\n(e) the efficiency of the scheme; and\n\n(f) the effectiveness of the scheme; and\n\n(g) any other matters the Minister considers relevant.\nA failure to comply with this subsection does not invalidate an\ninstrument made under subsection (1) authorising the scheme.\n\n(3) An instrument made under subsection (1) may make the\nauthorisation of the scheme subject to specified conditions.\n\n(4) An instrument made under subsection (1) authorising a scheme for\nwhich paragraph (1)(b) applies must set out the scheme.\n\n(5) More than one scheme may be authorised under subsection (1).",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58db-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "433a50dedcf548b73801b6da9e185497fa195d8cc2c0f256b3275e5151742a07"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58db",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58DB Minister may authorise external dispute resolution schemes",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 234,
      "values:originalText": "for a regulated sector\n\n(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, authorise an external\ndispute resolution scheme (an SPF EDR scheme) for the purposes\nof this Part and one or more regulated sectors if:\n\n(a) the scheme is already authorised under a Commonwealth law\nfor another purpose; or\n\n(b) the Minister is satisfied that the requirements prescribed by\nthe SPF rules for the purposes of subsection 58DC(1) are met\nfor the scheme.\nNote 1:\nFor paragraph (a), the Minister could, for example, authorise the\nAFCA scheme (within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001) to\napply for the purposes of this Part and a regulated sector. If that\nhappens, ASIC’s functions and powers relating to the AFCA scheme\n(for example, under section 1052A of that Act) will also apply for the\npurposes of this Part and the regulated sector.\nNote 2:\nFor variation and repeal, see subsection 33(3) of the Acts\nInterpretation Act 1901.\n\n(2) Before authorising a scheme, the Minister must consider:\n\n(a) the accessibility of the scheme; and\n\n(b) the independence of the scheme; and\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nExternal dispute resolution for the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 4\n\nSection 58DC\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\n(c) the fairness of the scheme; and\n\n(d) the accountability of the scheme; and\n\n(e) the efficiency of the scheme; and\n\n(f) the effectiveness of the scheme; and\n\n(g) any other matters the Minister considers relevant.\nA failure to comply with this subsection does not invalidate an\ninstrument made under subsection (1) authorising the scheme.\n\n(3) An instrument made under subsection (1) may make the\nauthorisation of the scheme subject to specified conditions.\n\n(4) An instrument made under subsection (1) authorising a scheme for\nwhich paragraph (1)(b) applies must set out the scheme.\n\n(5) More than one scheme may be authorised under subsection (1)."
    },
    {
