      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fc",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58FC Multiple remedies can be sought for a single contravention",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fc"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.248",
      "values:originalText": "Subject to section 58FM (about civil penalties), a provision of this\nDivision does not limit a court’s powers under any other provision\nof this Act or of any other Act.",
      "skos:definition": "Subject to section 58FM (about civil penalties), a provision of this\nDivision does not limit a court’s powers under any other provision\nof this Act or of any other Act.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fc-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "cbc56c36c211b189c2babc9fed40d51dffbd01102691c9cad1379936be44566f",
      "skos:note": "Court's powers under other provisions not limited"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fc",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58FC Multiple remedies can be sought for a single contravention",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 248,
      "values:originalText": "Subject to section 58FM (about civil penalties), a provision of this\nDivision does not limit a court’s powers under any other provision\nof this Act or of any other Act."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fc-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fc"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58FD Preference must be given to compensation for victims",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fd"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.248",
      "values:originalText": "If a court considers that:\n\n(a) it is appropriate to order a person (the defendant) to pay a\npecuniary penalty under an SPF civil penalty order in relation\nto a contravention or conduct; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate to order under Subdivision G the defendant\nto pay compensation to a person who has suffered loss or\ndamage as result of that contravention or conduct; and\n\n(c) the defendant does not have sufficient financial resources to\npay both the pecuniary penalty and the compensation;\nthe court must give preference to making an order for\ncompensation.\nSubdivision B—Monitoring or investigating compliance with an\nSPF code",
      "skos:definition": "If a court considers that:\n\n(a) it is appropriate to order a person (the defendant) to pay a\npecuniary penalty under an SPF civil penalty order in relation\nto a contravention or conduct; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate to order under Subdivision G the defendant\nto pay compensation to a person who has suffered loss or\ndamage as result of that contravention or conduct; and\n\n(c) the defendant does not have sufficient financial resources to\npay both the pecuniary penalty and the compensation;\nthe court must give preference to making an order for\ncompensation.\nSubdivision B—Monitoring or investigating compliance with an\nSPF code",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8b5b78aef25834764d0a7a11ea8d70e9192abb53db04b04adc48de542ff64627",
      "skos:note": "Court must give preference to compensation over pecuniary penalty if defendant cannot pay both."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fd",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58FD Preference must be given to compensation for victims",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 248,
      "values:originalText": "If a court considers that:\n\n(a) it is appropriate to order a person (the defendant) to pay a\npecuniary penalty under an SPF civil penalty order in relation\nto a contravention or conduct; and\n\n(b) it is appropriate to order under Subdivision G the defendant\nto pay compensation to a person who has suffered loss or\ndamage as result of that contravention or conduct; and\n\n(c) the defendant does not have sufficient financial resources to\npay both the pecuniary penalty and the compensation;\nthe court must give preference to making an order for\ncompensation.\nSubdivision B—Monitoring or investigating compliance with an\nSPF code"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fd-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Preference",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Court must prioritize compensation over pecuniary penalty.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "Defendant cannot pay both penalties.",
      "cml:conclusionText": "Compensation takes precedence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fe",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58FE Monitoring compliance with an SPF code—default",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fe"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.248",
      "values:originalText": "No alternative monitoring powers apply\n\n(1) This section applies for the SPF code for a regulated sector unless:\n\n(a) the ACMA, ASIC or the Commission is the SPF sector\nregulator for the sector; or\n\n(b) a declaration is in force under subsection 58FI(2) declaring\nthat provisions that include monitoring powers of the kind\nmentioned in subparagraph 58FI(1)(a)(i) apply in relation to\nprovisions of the SPF code.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FE\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nProvisions subject to monitoring\n\n(2) Each provision of the SPF code is subject to monitoring under\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether these provisions have been complied with. That\nPart includes powers of entry and inspection.\nInformation subject to monitoring\n\n(3) Information given in compliance or purported compliance with the\nSPF code is subject to monitoring under Part 2 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether the information is correct. It includes powers of\nentry and inspection.\nRelated provisions, authorised applicant, authorised person,\nissuing officer, relevant chief executive and relevant court\n\n(4) For the purposes of Part 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act, as that\nPart applies in relation to the provisions mentioned in\nsubsection (2) and the information mentioned in subsection (3):\n\n(a) there are no related provisions; and\n\n(b) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\napplicant; and\n\n(c) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\nperson; and\n\n(d) a magistrate is an issuing officer; and\n\n(e) the SPF sector regulator is the relevant chief executive; and\n\n(f) each of the following courts is a relevant court:\n\n(i) the Federal Court;\n\n(ii) the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2);\n\n(iii) a court of a State or Territory that has jurisdiction in\nrelation to the matter.\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.",
      "skos:definition": "No alternative monitoring powers apply\n\n(1) This section applies for the SPF code for a regulated sector unless:\n\n(a) the ACMA, ASIC or the Commission is the SPF sector\nregulator for the sector; or\n\n(b) a declaration is in force under subsection 58FI(2) declaring\nthat provisions that include monitoring powers of the kind\nmentioned in subparagraph 58FI(1)(a)(i) apply in relation to\nprovisions of the SPF code.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FE\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nProvisions subject to monitoring\n\n(2) Each provision of the SPF code is subject to monitoring under\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether these provisions have been complied with. That\nPart includes powers of entry and inspection.\nInformation subject to monitoring\n\n(3) Information given in compliance or purported compliance with the\nSPF code is subject to monitoring under Part 2 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether the information is correct. It includes powers of\nentry and inspection.\nRelated provisions, authorised applicant, authorised person,\nissuing officer, relevant chief executive and relevant court\n\n(4) For the purposes of Part 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act, as that\nPart applies in relation to the provisions mentioned in\nsubsection (2) and the information mentioned in subsection (3):\n\n(a) there are no related provisions; and\n\n(b) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\napplicant; and\n\n(c) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\nperson; and\n\n(d) a magistrate is an issuing officer; and\n\n(e) the SPF sector regulator is the relevant chief executive; and\n\n(f) each of the following courts is a relevant court:\n\n(i) the ",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fe-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fe-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fe-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fe-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "27ede31f95203e47ff11e2df678aa5a2f6d02b33a74fcbcfe8f36c5a3a284844"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fe",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58FE Monitoring compliance with an SPF code—default",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 248,
      "values:originalText": "No alternative monitoring powers apply\n\n(1) This section applies for the SPF code for a regulated sector unless:\n\n(a) the ACMA, ASIC or the Commission is the SPF sector\nregulator for the sector; or\n\n(b) a declaration is in force under subsection 58FI(2) declaring\nthat provisions that include monitoring powers of the kind\nmentioned in subparagraph 58FI(1)(a)(i) apply in relation to\nprovisions of the SPF code.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FE\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nProvisions subject to monitoring\n\n(2) Each provision of the SPF code is subject to monitoring under\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether these provisions have been complied with. That\nPart includes powers of entry and inspection.\nInformation subject to monitoring\n\n(3) Information given in compliance or purported compliance with the\nSPF code is subject to monitoring under Part 2 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act.\nNote:\nPart 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act creates a framework for\nmonitoring whether the information is correct. It includes powers of\nentry and inspection.\nRelated provisions, authorised applicant, authorised person,\nissuing officer, relevant chief executive and relevant court\n\n(4) For the purposes of Part 2 of the Regulatory Powers Act, as that\nPart applies in relation to the provisions mentioned in\nsubsection (2) and the information mentioned in subsection (3):\n\n(a) there are no related provisions; and\n\n(b) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\napplicant; and\n\n(c) an inspector of the SPF sector regulator is an authorised\nperson; and\n\n(d) a magistrate is an issuing officer; and\n\n(e) the SPF sector regulator is the relevant chief executive; and\n\n(f) each of the following courts is a relevant court:\n\n(i) the Federal Court;\n\n(ii) the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2);\n\n(iii) a court of a State or Territory that has jurisdiction in\nrelation to the matter.\n\nChapter 4  Provisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc."
    },
