    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fj",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58FJ Civil penalty provisions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fj"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.256",
      "values:originalText": "Enforcing civil penalty provisions\n\n(1) Each of the following is enforceable under Part 4 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act:\n\n(a) a civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\n\n(b) a civil penalty provision of an SPF code.\nNote:\nPart 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act allows a civil penalty provision to\nbe enforced by obtaining an order for a person to pay a pecuniary\npenalty for the contravention of the provision.\nAuthorised applicant\n\n(2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act:\n\n(a) the SPF general regulator is an authorised applicant in\nrelation to each civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\nand\n\n(b) the SPF sector regulator for a regulated sector is an\nauthorised applicant in relation to each civil penalty\nprovision of the SPF code for the sector.\nRelevant court\n\n(3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act, each of\nthe following courts is a relevant court in relation to each provision\nreferred to in subsection (1):\n\n(a) the Federal Court;\n\n(b) the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2);\n\n(c) a court of a State or Territory that has jurisdiction in relation\nto the matter.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FK\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026",
      "skos:definition": "Enforcing civil penalty provisions\n\n(1) Each of the following is enforceable under Part 4 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act:\n\n(a) a civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\n\n(b) a civil penalty provision of an SPF code.\nNote:\nPart 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act allows a civil penalty provision to\nbe enforced by obtaining an order for a person to pay a pecuniary\npenalty for the contravention of the provision.\nAuthorised applicant\n\n(2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act:\n\n(a) the SPF general regulator is an authorised applicant in\nrelation to each civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\nand\n\n(b) the SPF sector regulator for a regulated sector is an\nauthorised applicant in relation to each civil penalty\nprovision of the SPF code for the sector.\nRelevant court\n\n(3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act, each of\nthe following courts is a relevant court in relation to each provision\nreferred to in subsection (1):\n\n(a) the Federal Court;\n\n(b) the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2);\n\n(c) a court of a State or Territory that has jurisdiction in relation\nto the matter.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FK\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fj-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fj-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-58fj-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "62f3e12d9aa28d5c812fc97adedc9f3f2090ce7cff88ba17e98ba40ac873ed47"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-58fj",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58FJ Civil penalty provisions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 256,
      "values:originalText": "Enforcing civil penalty provisions\n\n(1) Each of the following is enforceable under Part 4 of the Regulatory\nPowers Act:\n\n(a) a civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\n\n(b) a civil penalty provision of an SPF code.\nNote:\nPart 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act allows a civil penalty provision to\nbe enforced by obtaining an order for a person to pay a pecuniary\npenalty for the contravention of the provision.\nAuthorised applicant\n\n(2) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act:\n\n(a) the SPF general regulator is an authorised applicant in\nrelation to each civil penalty provision of an SPF principle;\nand\n\n(b) the SPF sector regulator for a regulated sector is an\nauthorised applicant in relation to each civil penalty\nprovision of the SPF code for the sector.\nRelevant court\n\n(3) For the purposes of Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act, each of\nthe following courts is a relevant court in relation to each provision\nreferred to in subsection (1):\n\n(a) the Federal Court;\n\n(b) the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2);\n\n(c) a court of a State or Territory that has jurisdiction in relation\nto the matter.\n\nProvisions relating to particular industries, payment surcharges etc.  Chapter 4\nScams Prevention Framework  Part IVF\nEnforcing the Scams Prevention Framework  Division 6\n\nSection 58FK\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026"
    },
