    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-60c",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "60C Price exploitation in relation to the carbon tax repeal",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-60c"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.295",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An entity must not engage in price exploitation in relation to the\ncarbon tax repeal.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, an entity engages in price\nexploitation in relation to the carbon tax repeal if, and only if:\n\n(a) it makes a regulated supply; and\n\n(b) the price for the supply does not pass through all of the\nentity’s cost savings relating to the supply that are directly or\nindirectly attributable to the carbon tax repeal.\n\n(3) For the purposes of this Part, in determining whether the price for a\nsupply made by an entity does not pass through all of the entity’s\ncost savings relating to the supply that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal, have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) the entity’s cost savings that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal;\n\n(b) how the cost savings mentioned in paragraph (a) can\nreasonably be attributed to the different supplies that the\nentity makes;\n\n(c) the entity’s costs;\n\n(d) any other relevant matter that may reasonably influence the\nprice.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An entity must not engage in price exploitation in relation to the\ncarbon tax repeal.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, an entity engages in price\nexploitation in relation to the carbon tax repeal if, and only if:\n\n(a) it makes a regulated supply; and\n\n(b) the price for the supply does not pass through all of the\nentity’s cost savings relating to the supply that are directly or\nindirectly attributable to the carbon tax repeal.\n\n(3) For the purposes of this Part, in determining whether the price for a\nsupply made by an entity does not pass through all of the entity’s\ncost savings relating to the supply that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal, have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) the entity’s cost savings that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal;\n\n(b) how the cost savings mentioned in paragraph (a) can\nreasonably be attributed to the different supplies that the\nentity makes;\n\n(c) the entity’s costs;\n\n(d) any other relevant matter that may reasonably influence the\nprice.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-60c-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-60c-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-60c-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "46a04e481062801c9f09c3effebe1143c50308687c9af996339eddae025b0d75"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-60c",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "60C Price exploitation in relation to the carbon tax repeal",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 295,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An entity must not engage in price exploitation in relation to the\ncarbon tax repeal.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, an entity engages in price\nexploitation in relation to the carbon tax repeal if, and only if:\n\n(a) it makes a regulated supply; and\n\n(b) the price for the supply does not pass through all of the\nentity’s cost savings relating to the supply that are directly or\nindirectly attributable to the carbon tax repeal.\n\n(3) For the purposes of this Part, in determining whether the price for a\nsupply made by an entity does not pass through all of the entity’s\ncost savings relating to the supply that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal, have regard to the following\nmatters:\n\n(a) the entity’s cost savings that are directly or indirectly\nattributable to the carbon tax repeal;\n\n(b) how the cost savings mentioned in paragraph (a) can\nreasonably be attributed to the different supplies that the\nentity makes;\n\n(c) the entity’s costs;\n\n(d) any other relevant matter that may reasonably influence the\nprice."
    },
