    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "87CB Application of Part",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87cb"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.372",
      "values:originalText": "(1) This Part applies to a claim (an apportionable claim) if the claim is\na claim for damages made under section 236 of the Australian\nConsumer Law for:\n\n(a) economic loss; or\n\n(b) damage to property;\ncaused by conduct that was done in a contravention of section 18\nof the Australian Consumer Law.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, there is a single apportionable claim\nin proceedings in respect of the same loss or damage even if the\nclaim for the loss or damage is based on more than one cause of\naction (whether or not of the same or a different kind).\n\n(3) In this Part, a concurrent wrongdoer, in relation to a claim, is a\nperson who is one of 2 or more persons whose acts or omissions\n(or act or omission) caused, independently of each other or jointly,\nthe damage or loss that is the subject of the claim.\n\n(4) For the purposes of this Part, apportionable claims are limited to\nthose claims specified in subsection (1).\n\n(5) For the purposes of this Part, it does not matter that a concurrent\nwrongdoer is insolvent, is being wound up or has ceased to exist or\ndied.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) This Part applies to a claim (an apportionable claim) if the claim is\na claim for damages made under section 236 of the Australian\nConsumer Law for:\n\n(a) economic loss; or\n\n(b) damage to property;\ncaused by conduct that was done in a contravention of section 18\nof the Australian Consumer Law.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, there is a single apportionable claim\nin proceedings in respect of the same loss or damage even if the\nclaim for the loss or damage is based on more than one cause of\naction (whether or not of the same or a different kind).\n\n(3) In this Part, a concurrent wrongdoer, in relation to a claim, is a\nperson who is one of 2 or more persons whose acts or omissions\n(or act or omission) caused, independently of each other or jointly,\nthe damage or loss that is the subject of the claim.\n\n(4) For the purposes of this Part, apportionable claims are limited to\nthose claims specified in subsection (1).\n\n(5) For the purposes of this Part, it does not matter that a concurrent\nwrongdoer is insolvent, is being wound up or has ceased to exist or\ndied.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87cb-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "5d5acab91d6d30b458a37ded2c111ca233bcd9d2d6e463a5e8d70a7689a1458f"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87cb",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "87CB Application of Part",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 372,
      "values:originalText": "(1) This Part applies to a claim (an apportionable claim) if the claim is\na claim for damages made under section 236 of the Australian\nConsumer Law for:\n\n(a) economic loss; or\n\n(b) damage to property;\ncaused by conduct that was done in a contravention of section 18\nof the Australian Consumer Law.\n\n(2) For the purposes of this Part, there is a single apportionable claim\nin proceedings in respect of the same loss or damage even if the\nclaim for the loss or damage is based on more than one cause of\naction (whether or not of the same or a different kind).\n\n(3) In this Part, a concurrent wrongdoer, in relation to a claim, is a\nperson who is one of 2 or more persons whose acts or omissions\n(or act or omission) caused, independently of each other or jointly,\nthe damage or loss that is the subject of the claim.\n\n(4) For the purposes of this Part, apportionable claims are limited to\nthose claims specified in subsection (1).\n\n(5) For the purposes of this Part, it does not matter that a concurrent\nwrongdoer is insolvent, is being wound up or has ceased to exist or\ndied."
    },
