    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "87G Date of discoverability",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87g"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.381",
      "values:originalText": "Definition\n\n(1) The date of discoverability for the death or injury is the first date\nwhen the plaintiff in the proceeding knows or ought to know each\nof the following:\n\n(a) that the death or personal injury has occurred;\n\n(b) that the death or personal injury was attributable to a\ncontravention of this Act;\n\n(c) that in the case of a personal injury—the injury was\nsignificant enough to justify bringing an action.\nHave 3 years elapsed since the date of discoverability?\nHas the long-stop period expired?\nPersonal injury damages\nmay be awarded\nNo\nDid the death or personal injury result\nfrom smoking or other use of tobacco\nproducts?\nYes\nNo\nPersonal injury damages\ncannot be awarded\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nChapter 5  Enforcement and remedies",
      "skos:definition": "Definition\n\n(1) The date of discoverability for the death or injury is the first date\nwhen the plaintiff in the proceeding knows or ought to know each\nof the following:\n\n(a) that the death or personal injury has occurred;\n\n(b) that the death or personal injury was attributable to a\ncontravention of this Act;\n\n(c) that in the case of a personal injury—the injury was\nsignificant enough to justify bringing an action.\nHave 3 years elapsed since the date of discoverability?\nHas the long-stop period expired?\nPersonal injury damages\nmay be awarded\nNo\nDid the death or personal injury result\nfrom smoking or other use of tobacco\nproducts?\nYes\nNo\nPersonal injury damages\ncannot be awarded\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nChapter 5  Enforcement and remedies",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "10c08a64fc2fdc28b068658962a4842835c185767636b3c76edcf44b7b33449e",
      "skos:note": "Defines date of discoverability for death or injury claims."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87g",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "87G Date of discoverability",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 381,
      "values:originalText": "Definition\n\n(1) The date of discoverability for the death or injury is the first date\nwhen the plaintiff in the proceeding knows or ought to know each\nof the following:\n\n(a) that the death or personal injury has occurred;\n\n(b) that the death or personal injury was attributable to a\ncontravention of this Act;\n\n(c) that in the case of a personal injury—the injury was\nsignificant enough to justify bringing an action.\nHave 3 years elapsed since the date of discoverability?\nHas the long-stop period expired?\nPersonal injury damages\nmay be awarded\nNo\nDid the death or personal injury result\nfrom smoking or other use of tobacco\nproducts?\nYes\nNo\nPersonal injury damages\ncannot be awarded\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nChapter 5  Enforcement and remedies"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87g-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "has",
      "cml:logicSummary": "States a condition",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:conclusionText": "The date of discoverability..."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87h",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "87H Long-stop period",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87h"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.383",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The long-stop period for the death or injury of a person is:\n\n(a) the period of 12 years following the act or omission alleged\nto have caused the death or injury; or\n\n(b) that period as extended by the court.\n\n(2) The court must not extend the period by more than 3 years beyond\nthe date of discoverability for the death or injury.\n\n(3) In considering whether to extend the period, the court must have\nregard to the justice of the case, and, in particular, must have\nregard to:\n\n(a) whether the passage of time has prejudiced a fair trial; and\n\n(b) the nature and extent of the person’s loss or damage; and\n\n(c) the nature of the defendant’s conduct alleged to have caused\nthe death or injury; and\n\n(d) the nature of the defendant’s conduct since the alleged act or\nomission.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The long-stop period for the death or injury of a person is:\n\n(a) the period of 12 years following the act or omission alleged\nto have caused the death or injury; or\n\n(b) that period as extended by the court.\n\n(2) The court must not extend the period by more than 3 years beyond\nthe date of discoverability for the death or injury.\n\n(3) In considering whether to extend the period, the court must have\nregard to the justice of the case, and, in particular, must have\nregard to:\n\n(a) whether the passage of time has prejudiced a fair trial; and\n\n(b) the nature and extent of the person’s loss or damage; and\n\n(c) the nature of the defendant’s conduct alleged to have caused\nthe death or injury; and\n\n(d) the nature of the defendant’s conduct since the alleged act or\nomission.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87h-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87h-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87h-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f2ec87735b31deb45a6e30ce9dc912ad11d68c78a47ae1dce2973ffef675df67"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-87h",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "87H Long-stop period",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 383,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The long-stop period for the death or injury of a person is:\n\n(a) the period of 12 years following the act or omission alleged\nto have caused the death or injury; or\n\n(b) that period as extended by the court.\n\n(2) The court must not extend the period by more than 3 years beyond\nthe date of discoverability for the death or injury.\n\n(3) In considering whether to extend the period, the court must have\nregard to the justice of the case, and, in particular, must have\nregard to:\n\n(a) whether the passage of time has prejudiced a fair trial; and\n\n(b) the nature and extent of the person’s loss or damage; and\n\n(c) the nature of the defendant’s conduct alleged to have caused\nthe death or injury; and\n\n(d) the nature of the defendant’s conduct since the alleged act or\nomission."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-87h-ss-1",
